-- EXPECT-ERROR: ActorCap
-- R8/R2 — A PROCESS CAPABILITY IS NOT IO AUTHORITY (2026-08-06).
--
-- Found by the R8 audit. `get_cap : Pid(a) -> Option(Cap(a))` shared the `Cap`
-- constructor with IO capabilities, so whenever `a` was FREE it unified with
-- `IO` and handed the caller root authority. This program typechecked with
-- exit 0 — in a module whose `main` takes NO parameter and is therefore
-- granted nothing at all.
--
-- The route matters, and getting it right took a correction. `spawn` returns
-- `Pid[state]`, which PINS `a` to the actor's state record, so the ordinary
-- actor path was already safe (`expected IO but got { n : Int }`). `self()`
-- is not a `Pid` at all. The vector is `pid_of_int : Int -> Pid(a)`, whose
-- `a` is free — it exists so supervisors can rebuild a Pid from a state
-- field, and is documented as unsafe. Free `a` plus a shared constructor is
-- what produced authority from an integer.
--
-- That defeated R2. R2's entire content is that the root capability is
-- GRANTED to `main`, not taken; here it was taken from an integer literal.
-- It also made the sentence R2 earned ("a capability can only be received")
-- false.
--
-- The cause was not a missing check. `Cap` named TWO unrelated things that
-- shared one type constructor and therefore unified:
--
--   IO capability       VUnit, fully erased, compile-time only, governed by
--                       `needs` + the lattice + R2/R3/R4
--   process capability  VCap(pid, epoch), a real runtime value with epoch
--                       validation, consumed by send_checked/revoke_cap/
--                       is_cap_valid, governed by actor liveness
--
-- Process capabilities are now `ActorCap(a)`, so there is nothing to unify.
-- A sweep would have patched this one symptom and left the next builtin
-- returning `Cap(a)` to reopen it.
--
-- Accept companion: accept/t150, the legitimate process-capability flow.
mod ProcCapIsNotIO do
  needs IO

  pfn wants_root(r : Cap(IO)) : Int do 1 end

  fn main() do
    match get_cap(pid_of_int(1)) do
      Some(c) -> println(int_to_string(wants_root(c)))
      None    -> println("none")
    end
  end
end
